# .publicignore
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blocklist consumed by the /mirror-public skill via `rsync --exclude-from=`.
# Every pattern here is a path (relative to the repo root) that MUST NEVER be
# copied into the public mirror repo (https://github.com/samwelmunga/jenga-npm).
#
# Policy: over-block, don't under-block. Anything missed is a private leak;
# anything extra is merely an unshipped file. Add new private paths as they
# appear — this file is the single source of truth for public-mirror scope.
#
# Rule (user, 2026-09-10): a test may never outlive its subject in the mirror.
# If a test exercises a skill that is not available publicly, it must EITHER be
# blocklisted here alongside that subject, OR be conditioned to skip when the
# subject is absent. Choosing neither used to produce a test that passes
# privately and fails only in the public mirror, found by a stage-deploy gate
# rather than by anyone running tests. As of E28_S16 this is gate-enforced:
# skills/j-mirror-public/scripts/mirror.sh's check_test_subject_invariant runs
# the real shipping suite in a mirror-shaped tree before every real push and
# refuses to push a stranded test. See project/documentation/public-mirror-content-parity.md,
# "Rule: a test may never outlive its subject in the mirror", for the two
# sanctioned authoring options (still correct and unchanged) and the list of
# skills that are never public in either naming form.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Private scrum board (epics, stories, tasks, indices) ---
project/board/

# --- Inter-agent queues (triggers, handoff files, update proposals) ---
project/queue/

# --- Runtime event logs ---
# publish-history.json is the /publish ledger and MUST ship: publishing runs
# from the public repo, so the ledger has to live there. Two rsync rules make
# this work and both are load-bearing: include lines must precede the exclude
# (first match wins), and the directory itself must be included because rsync
# will not descend into an excluded directory to reach a file inside it.
+ project/logs/
+ project/logs/publish-history.json
- project/logs/**

# --- E73_S01 spike harness and its raw hook-payload logs ---
# Throwaway research tooling. The raw logs can contain prompt text, so they are named explicitly even
# though `- project/logs/**` above already covers them (belt and braces: a later change to that rule
# must not silently expose them).
project/logs/spike-hook-payloads/
project/spikes/E73_S01/

# --- Problem / security / conflict rapports ---
project/rapports/

# --- Top-level todo (working list of pending missions) ---
project/todo.md

# --- Private planning + execution artefacts tied to board items ---
project/documentation/plans/
project/documentation/summaries/
project/instructions/

# --- Unposted draft reply for the maintainer to post by hand on the public issue ---
project/documentation/credential-access-issue-reply-draft.md

# --- Project-local playbooks (E28_S14_T01, user decision 2026-09-17) ---
# `project/.playbooks/` is per-project by definition — `load-playbooks.sh` scans it as the PROJECT
# source alongside the framework-owned `skills/jenga/playbooks/` BUILTIN source (E53_S09_T01).
# Ours are not framework content, and a consumer's are not ours to overwrite. Shipping our own
# project-local playbooks would push private workflow tooling downstream as if it were part of the
# framework, and would collide with a consumer's own `project/.playbooks/` on their side.
#
# This closes an unintentional npm/GitHub asymmetry: `project/.playbooks/improve-to-commit.json` is
# tracked in the public mirror today but is absent from `package.json`'s `files` array, so people
# cloning the public GitHub repo received it while npm consumers never did. It was neither a
# deliberate worked example nor properly private. Closed in the private direction.
#
# Safety per the "a test may never outlive its subject" rule above — re-verified 2026-09-19:
# tests/load-playbooks-project-source.bats and tests/load-playbooks-resolve-lookup.bats build their
# fixtures under $BATS_TEST_TMPDIR and never read the real directory; no file under tests/, scripts/
# or skills/ references `improve-to-commit` by name, and the only mentions anywhere outside the
# private project/ tree are the explanatory prose in this block and in
# project/documentation/public-mirror-content-parity.md, which depend on nothing and strand no test; and
# load-playbooks.sh treats a missing
# `project/.playbooks/` as a SILENT no-op (no warning, no error). So no test is stranded and the
# mirror simply has no PROJECT playbook source.
#
# This does NOT delete or relocate improve-to-commit — it keeps working normally in the private
# repo, it simply stops shipping. rsync --delete purges the already-present mirror copy on the next
# /j-mirror-public run (E28_S04).
project/.playbooks/

# --- Project-local workflows (E80_S01_T08) ---
# `project/.workflows/` is per-project by definition -- it is the PROJECT source for workflow
# definitions, alongside the framework-owned `templates/workflows/` BUILTIN source, which DOES ship.
# Ours are not framework content, and a consumer's are not ours to overwrite: shipping our own would
# push private workflow tooling downstream as if it were part of the framework, and would land in the
# same directory a consumer uses for their own workflows. Same reasoning as `project/.playbooks/`
# above.
#
# No test is stranded: `load-workflows.sh` treats a missing `project/.workflows/` as a SILENT no-op
# (no warning, no error), so the mirror simply has no PROJECT workflow source. As with the playbook
# entry, no `.claude/` or `.agents/` parallel lines are needed -- those mirrors are generated from the
# root, and the project-local directory is not one of the root framework directories they receive.
project/.workflows/
#
# --- Decision (E80_S05_T07, 2026-10-25): j.workflow SHIPS to the public mirror ---
# The user did not ask to keep the skill private, and it is framework-generic: j.workflow is the runner
# for the workflow engine (E80 Purpose), the same way j.playbook is the runner for playbooks. So there
# is deliberately NO ignore entry for `skills/j-workflow/` or `scripts/workflow/`; both ship, as do the
# framework-owned `templates/workflows/` builtins and `scripts/workflow/examples/`. Only the per-project
# `project/.workflows/` source above is private. The npm `files` array already covers `skills/`,
# `scripts/` and `templates/`, so the two channels agree (no npm/GitHub asymmetry).
#
# Stranded-check outcome (2026-10-25, via scripts/check-publicignore-match.sh, the same rsync matcher
# /j-mirror-public uses): all 25 of skills/j-workflow/{SKILL.md,scripts/workflow-cli.sh}, every
# scripts/workflow/*.sh, scripts/workflow/examples/*, templates/workflows/example-report.{json,md},
# and the skills the skill text names (skills/j-playbook/SKILL.md, skills/jenga/SKILL.md) classify
# PUBLIC. The only shipped example workflow (templates/workflows/example-report.json) uses compute and
# render steps only, with no skill step, so it cannot reference a private-only skill. Every test that
# touches project/.workflows/ builds it in a temp fixture root, so none is stranded by the entry above.
# Nothing was ignored as a result. tests/workflow-skill-registration.bats asserts this decision.
# Note: before changing this decision, re-run the check above and update that test.

# --- Workflow run state (E80_S02_T02) ---
# `project/data/workflow-runs/` holds the private, session-spanning state of workflow runs (state
# files plus the lock and atomic-write leftovers beside them). It is git-ignored apart from its
# `.gitkeep`, but that placeholder is tracked, so without this entry the directory would ship. The
# mirror already blocks `project/queue/`, yet `project/data/` is not blocked (it holds shipped,
# tracked files such as `baselines.json`), so only this subdirectory is listed, never `project/data/`.
project/data/workflow-runs/

# --- Workflow artifacts (E80_S04_T11) ---
# `project/data/workflow-artifacts/` holds the published artifacts of workflow runs (sprint plans,
# roadmaps). It is tracked in git on purpose, so a collaborator or the dashboard timeline can read it
# after a fresh clone, but the content is project-specific planning, which must not leak downstream
# (same reasoning as `project/board/`). `project/data/` is not blocked as a whole (it holds shipped,
# tracked files such as `baselines.json`), so only this subdirectory is listed.
project/data/workflow-artifacts/

